half — verbatim *"can we limit the amount of threads it uses? I was just surprised it took so long. I woke up in the middle of the night to it."* → the run was re-queued with `--workers=3` + `nice -n 19` baked in. **A second runner at normal priority would starve a deliberately-niced run and re-create exactly the saturation the owner ruled against hours earlier.** 🎯 **THE CORRECTION, and it is the reusable half: THE HOLD WAS KEYED TO A CLOCK, AND THE CLOCK UNDER-STATES THE WINDOW.** s1665 handed off *"should land ~19:35"*. That estimate is wrong twice. **(a) Arithmetically** — re-measured from the live log, 2,254@18:44 → 2,352@19:00 → 2,442@19:10, i.e. ~9/min with 362 executions left, so the PARALLEL arm alone lands ≈19:50. **(b) Structurally, which is the part no better arithmetic fixes** — the parallel arm is not the end of the load-sensitive window. **Scope 5 requires re-running every failing spec FILE serially (`--workers=1`) after the full run, and that serial arm IS the discriminator between load-attributable and real reds** (451 failures observed at last read). A test that is load-attributable but *also* fails serially because a fire added load during the re-run is classified **REAL** and enters the inventory as a fresh excused red — which F-1643-2's own pricing calls *"strictly worse than the 13-day-old one it replaced."* **A fire that reads "~19:35", waits until 19:40 and dispatches would do the right thing at the wrong moment and silently poison the snapshot.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING FOR THE NEXT FIRES — KEY THE HOLD ON THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE CLOCK: dispatch `f1662-2` when `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs lane-a` stops saying BUSY, and not before.** ✓ **VERIFIED IN THE CODE, not assumed:** `lane-usable.mjs:292` derives BUSY from `tasks/running/<slot>.pid` (live: `tasks/running/lane-a.pid` = 61900) via `isSlotBusy`, which fails safe to BUSY on anything it cannot establish (`:279–:287`). The runner holds that pidfile for the **whole** task, scope 5 included —